      "sha256": "693e030d6669b698bc23a94085d538bf95977b97a6b5fe9fbdc320758e6699bc",
      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of two common ways to serve a single large model locally:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs \u2014 one logical model instance, weights and activations sharded across 4 GPUs.\n- Option B: 4 independent 1-GPU replicas behind a router \u2014 four full copies of the model, each on its own GPU, with a load balancer/router in front.\n\nI\u2019ll walk through the dimensions you asked about, then give a decision guide.\n\n1) Latency\n\n- TP across 4 GPUs\n  - Time-to-first-token (TTFT): Often lower for large models because the forward pass is split across GPUs; each GPU does a fraction of the matmuls, and the result is combined via all-reduce. If interconnect is fast (NVLink), the added communication overhead is modest.\n  - Inter-token latency (decode): Each token still requires an all-reduce (or similar) across GPUs. With NVLink this is usually a few microseconds to low tens of microseconds per layer; with PCIe it can be a significant fraction of the compute time, especially for small batch sizes.\n  - Net: For a single request or small batches, TP can give lower TTFT and acceptable inter-token latency if the interconnect is good.\n\n- 4 independent 1-GPU replicas\n  - TTFT: Each replica must run the full forward pass on one GPU. If the model fits comfortably in one GPU\u2019s memory and compute, TTFT can be very low. If the model is near the edge of what one GPU can handle, TTFT may be higher due to memory bandwidth limits or even offloading.\n  - Inter-token latency: No cross-GPU communication per token; each token is generated entirely on one GPU. This can be very fast if the GPU has high memory bandwidth and the model fits well.\n  - Net: For small batches, replicas often have lower per-token latency because there\u2019s no synchronization overhead. However, if the model is too large for one GPU, this option may not be feasible or may require offloading, which hurts latency.\n\n2) Throughput\n\n- TP across 4 GPUs\n  - Throughput scales sub-linearly with the number of GPUs due to communication overhead. For large batch sizes, the compute-bound nature of the workload means you can still get good throughput, but not 4x.\n  - Best when you need to serve a model that doesn\u2019t fit on",
      "text_preview": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of two common ways to serve a single large model locally:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs \u2014 one logical model instance, weights and activations sharded across 4 GPUs.\n- Option B: 4 independent 1-GPU replicas behind a router \u2014 four full copies of the model, each on its ",
