      "stream_token_id_count": 510,
      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a technical guide for adding a new benchmark to an existing optimization repository. This is a practical, structured document. Let me think about what makes a good optimization benchmark repo and what the conventions should be.\n\nKey sections to cover:\n1. Where to put scripts, data, notes, patches, result summaries\n2. How to name run directories\n3. What to record for reproducibility\n4. How to decide whether a result is valid enough to promote\n\nLet me think about a sensible directory structure and conventions that would work for a real optimization repo. I'll make this practical and specific, not vague. I'll use a concrete example to ground it.\n\nLet me structure this as a proper technical guide with clear sections, code blocks for directory structures and examples, and actionable criteria. I'll aim for something that feels like it was written by someone who has actually maintained such a repo and hit the pain points.\n\nLet me think about the directory layout:\n- benchmarks/ (top-level)\n  - <benchmark-name>/\n    - scripts/ (the actual benchmark code)\n    - data/ (input data, or scripts to generate it)\n    - notes/ (design notes, observations)\n    - patches/ (patches to the system under test)\n    - results/ (run directories)\n    - README.md (overview)\n    - results_summary.md (curated summary)\n\nRun directory naming: something like YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-<short-desc> or a sequential number with a tag.\n\nReproducibility: environment, versions, hardware, compiler flags, seed, data version, full command line.\n\nValidity criteria: statistical significance, warmup, multiple runs, no contamination, baseline comparison, etc.\n\nLet me write this as a clean, well-structured guide.\n</think>\n\n# Adding a New Benchmark to the Optimization Repo\n\n## 1. Directory Layout\n\nEvery benchmark lives under `benchmarks/<name>/` using `kebab-case`. Create the full skeleton before writing any code:\n\n```\nbenchmarks/\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 <name>/\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 README.md              # 1-paragraph purpose, link to issue/ticket\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 scripts/\n    \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 run.sh             # single entry point; see \u00a73\n    \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 <helper>.py        # benchmark logic, data prep, etc.\n    \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 plot.py            # optional: generate figures from",
