      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a risk register for switching a production inference endpoint from one runtime to another. This is a very specific technical/operational task. They want at least 8 risks with: severity, likelihood, detection method, mitigation, and rollback trigger. They explicitly want real operational and quality risks, not generic PM filler.\n\nLet me think about what actually goes wrong when you switch inference runtimes in production. I need to think like an ML engineer / SRE who has actually done this.\n\nReal risks when switching inference runtimes (e.g., from TensorFlow Serving to Triton, from TorchServe to a custom FastAPI+ONNX, from vLLM to TGI, from one GPU driver stack to another, from CPU to GPU, from one quantization format to another):\n\n1. **Numerical precision drift / output divergence** - Different runtimes compute the same model slightly differently due to floating-point ordering, kernel implementations, cuDNN vs cuBLAS paths, different reduction orders. This can cause subtle output changes that shift classification boundaries or degrade generation quality. Detection: A/B shadow traffic comparing outputs. Rollback trigger: KL divergence or cosine similarity drops below threshold.\n\n2. **Latency regression (p99/p999)** - The new runtime might have different batching behavior, different kernel launch overhead, different memory allocation patterns. p50 might be fine but p999 could be 3x worse due to different memory management or synchronization points. Detection: Canary deployment with SLO dashboards. Rollback trigger: p999 exceeds SLO for N consecutive minutes.\n\n3. **GPU memory leak / OOM under sustained load** - The new runtime might have a memory leak in its allocator, or different memory fragmentation patterns. Under sustained production load (not the 10-minute test), GPU memory creeps up until OOM. Detection: GPU memory monitoring (nvidia-smi, DCGM). Rollback trigger: GPU memory growth rate exceeds threshold over 30 min window.\n\n4. **Batching / concurrency behavior change** - Old runtime might do dynamic batching with a 5ms window; new runtime might do static batching or have a different max batch size. Under bursty traffic, this changes throughput characteristics and can cause request queuing that wasn't there before. Detection: Queue depth monitoring, request-to-response time distribution comparison. Rollback trigger: Queue depth exceeds threshold or throughput drops below X% of baseline.\n\n5.",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a risk register for switching a production inference endpoint from one runtime to another. This is a very specific technical/operational task. They want at least 8 risks with: severity, likelihood, detection method, mitigation, and rollback trigger. They explicitly want real operational and qua",
