      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user is asking me to write a decision memo about whether to keep optimizing a current model or switch to a different model family. This is a technical/strategic document that should be structured as a proper memo. Let me think about what makes a good decision memo in this context:\n\n1. It needs to be specific enough to be useful but general enough that it reads as a real memo (I'll frame it around a plausible scenario - let's say an NLP/ML team working on a classification or generation task)\n2. It should include all the requested sections: current evidence, opportunity cost, criteria for stopping, expected next experiments, quality constraints, and artifacts to preserve\n3. The tone should be professional, decisive, and balanced - not wishy-washy\n4. It should feel like a real internal document with a clear recommendation\n\nLet me structure this as a proper memo with:\n- Header (To, From, Date, Subject)\n- Executive summary / recommendation\n- Current evidence (what we know, metrics, diminishing returns)\n- Opportunity cost analysis\n- Criteria for stopping (when do we stop optimizing the current model)\n- Expected next experiments (what we'd try if we switch)\n- Quality constraints (what we can't compromise on)\n- Artifacts to preserve\n- Decision and next steps\n\nI'll make it feel real by using specific (but fictional) metrics, model names, and team references. Let me make the scenario: a team working on a document classification/routing system, currently using a fine-tuned BERT-based model, considering switching to a larger transformer family (like a Llama-based or Mistral-based approach). Actually, let me make it more generic but specific enough - a fine-tuned encoder model vs. a decoder-only LLM approach for a text classification task.\n\nLet me write this as a complete, well-structured memo.\n</think>\n\n# Decision Memo: Continue Optimizing Current Model vs. Switch Model Family\n\n**To:** ML Platform Team, Product Engineering, Data Science Leadership\n**From:** [Lead ML Engineer], Applied ML Group\n**Date:** 14 June 2025\n**Re:** Go/No-Go on further optimization of `doc-router-v3` (fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base) vs. migration to a decoder-only LLM family (Mistral-7B / Llama-3-8B class)\n**Classification:** Internal \u2013",
